{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-77321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{7})=(-16.601\\,5…)^{∘}≈-{16.602}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{16.602}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={343.398}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={343.398}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={16.602}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/c86fca87f7f46c0155be358f377550a4adebf1a2040c874d55c00473766745f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{16.602}^{∘} \\\\  & ={196.602}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈343.398\\cdot 196.602=67\\,513\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eebd64fdadde1a442a245a253a46d67d738f98d9edec164e940af87c612db4a1"} {"question" "q-77321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "34\\,364"] ["math" "52\\,364"] ["math" "67\\,513"] ["math" "76\\,432"] ["math" "86\\,234"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77321" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "67\\,513"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/00d540b1f6412e4af2617452fbf0c58750d01e844d89d0d92cfacc0cb3e45e32.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eebd64fdadde1a442a245a253a46d67d738f98d9edec164e940af87c612db4a1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77789" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-2$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})(-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eebd64fdadde1a442a245a253a46d67d738f98d9edec164e940af87c612db4a1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{6{\\pi }^{2}}{25}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+30=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-12$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{3}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation II has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eebd64fdadde1a442a245a253a46d67d738f98d9edec164e940af87c612db4a1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-77812" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{10})=(-17.457\\,603…)^{∘}≈-{17.458}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{17.458}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={342.542}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={342.542}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/assets/821713cb7b71b2b15da5ecdf90c0e836234d280fa3753c71615b570017773672.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{17.458}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197.458}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{197}^{∘},\\,x≈{343}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "eebd64fdadde1a442a245a253a46d67d738f98d9edec164e940af87c612db4a1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "197"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"} {"question" "q-338755" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "343"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13989101/state.json" "file_sha256" "c013d42ca397be0ef3726726193cb21f216c60ea875d48af1867571aea03d2e1"}] "usage" {} "basis" 997 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
